 intro='Read a derivative as a prediction of change. Build gradients through compositions, distinguish slope from curvature, and check a numerical implementation without mistaking a check for a proof.',
 sections=[('A derivative predicts change', r'For a differentiable scalar loss, \(f(x+h)=f(x)+f^{\prime}(x)h+o(|h|)\). The derivative has units of loss per parameter unit; its sign predicts sufficiently small changes.'),
 ('Directions, not only coordinates', r'Total differentiability gives \(f(\theta+h)=f(\theta)+\nabla f(\theta)^Th+o(\|h\|)\). The dot product is a local change prediction, and the steepest direction depends on the chosen norm.'),
 ('Differentiate the whole computation', r'Composition carries changes forward and sensitivities backward. For \(\theta=e^\beta\), \(d\ell/d\beta=\theta\,d\ell/d\theta\). A shared parameter in a recurrence contributes at every occurrence.'),
 ('Curvature and numerical checks', r'The Hessian predicts the second-order term \(h^THh/2\). Positive curvature on coordinate axes alone does not establish a minimum. Central differences balance truncation and cancellation; they do not prove a derivative correct.')],
 example=r'For \(\ell(\theta)=(\theta-3)^2/2\), at θ=1 a step h=1 has predicted change −2, exact change −3/2, and curvature correction 1/2.',
